From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 23:14:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] fix(gastown): stop patrol wisps adopting themselves as
 their own successor
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0003 replaced the unconditional pour with "reuse the first open
assigned wisp", but never excluded the wisp the cycle is about to burn.
A poured+assigned wisp stays `open` until something claims it, and
GC_BEAD_ID is exported only by the convergence runner — so on a fresh
session CURRENT_WISP resolved empty, the live wisp was adopted as NEXT,
any genuine successor was burned as "surplus", and step 4 then had
nothing to burn: `gc runtime drain-ack; exit 1` on every cycle.

Because namedWorkReady re-wakes any session with an assigned bead, that
is a self-sustaining wake/drain loop. Observed live 2026-07-24: 80
stop/wake cycles at ~40s intervals across deacon and witness.

Two changes at each site:

  - CURRENT_WISP now matches `in_progress,open` and prefers in_progress,
    then oldest, so a never-claimed wisp still resolves.
  - OPEN_WISPS excludes $CURRENT_WISP, so the wisp being burned can no
    longer be mistaken for its own replacement.

10 CURRENT_WISP + 10 OPEN_WISPS sites across the deacon/refinery/witness
formulas and prompt templates. mol-witness-patrol.toml is intentionally
untouched: it has no CURRENT_WISP variable (it burns via an LLM-supplied
<this-wisp-id> placeholder), so the exclusion has nothing to reference
there — that formula needs a separate structural fix.

Refs: gc-a6d, gc-p0j
